{% extends 'dashboard/help/_topic.html.twig' %}
{% block content %}
<p>catto-mail reads its configuration from <code>infra/.env</code> on the server (<code>/home/cattomail/catto-mail/infra/.env</code> after the installer).
The file holds only the settings an operator sets, grouped under headings; everything else (container names, ports inside the topology, paths, database role names) is built in.
Every setting is described in <code>docs/contracts/environment.md</code>.</p>

<h2>Changing a setting in the dashboard</h2>
<p><strong>System › Settings</strong> lists the operational settings: pacing, validation, webhooks, API and client limits, reputation thresholds, retention, the time zone, the log level and, in production, backups and certificate renewal.
For each one it shows the value in <code>infra/.env</code>, the value set in the dashboard and the value in force.</p>
<ul>
<li>A value set in the dashboard is stored in the database and <strong>takes precedence over <code>infra/.env</code> without changing the file</strong>. Empty the field to use the file's value again.</li>
<li>Every change needs a reason and is recorded in the audit log with the old and the new value. Changing settings needs the permission <code>SYSTEM.SETTINGS.MANAGE</code> (administrators).</li>
<li>A change takes effect when it is <strong>applied</strong>. In production, <em>Apply changes</em> asks the host agent to render the configuration again with the dashboard values and to restart the services that use the changed settings (<code>smarthostctl prod settings-apply</code>). In development, run <code>infra/bin/smarthostctl settings-apply</code>.</li>
<li>The renderer checks every dashboard value with the same rules as the page. A value that breaks a rule is ignored and shown as <em>ignored</em>; if the values together break a rule of the configuration, none is applied and the page says why. A bad value never stops the services.</li>
<li>Every later render (recreate, upgrade) uses the dashboard values too, so they survive restarts and upgrades.</li>
</ul>
<p>Secrets, the database, networks and ports, certificates and keys, host names, domains and addresses, the live-delivery switch and the SMTP probe switch are set only in <code>infra/.env</code>: the services need them before they can read the database, or they have their own audited controls.</p>
<p>If the dashboard is unreachable, list or clear the dashboard values on the server: <code>infra/bin/smarthostctl prod console smarthost:settings list</code> and <code>... smarthost:settings reset NAME --operator you@example.com --reason "..."</code>, then <code>smarthostctl prod settings-apply</code>.</p>

<h2>The time zone</h2>
<p><code>SMARTHOST_TIMEZONE</code> (default <code>Africa/Johannesburg</code>, SAST, UTC+2) is the installation's time zone. Every time catto-mail shows, writes or computes is in it: the dashboard, API responses and webhook payloads (with the zone's offset, for example <code>2026-10-10T10:48:56.123456+02:00</code>), exports, the logs of every service, the database sessions, the Postfix log, snapshot and backup names, and the calendar days and months of quotas, usage and billing.
The installer sets the server's own time zone to it as well, so the journal and the timers use it; the host check in Diagnostics warns when they differ (fix: <code>sudo timedatectl set-timezone Africa/Johannesburg</code> as root).
Changing the time zone restarts every service when it is applied.</p>
<p>Fields whose format a standard fixes keep it: HTTP date headers are always GMT, and <code>Smarthost-Signature</code> carries Unix time.</p>

<h2>Older configuration files</h2>
<p>Before 0.2.3 <code>infra/.env</code> listed every variable with a comment naming its phase. <code>smarthostctl prod upgrade</code> rewrites it in the grouped layout and keeps the old file beside it as <code>infra/.env.v&lt;version&gt;-&lt;date and time&gt;</code>; a rollback to that version restores it. To do it by hand: <code>infra/bin/smarthostctl prod env-migrate</code>.</p>
